+Rendered-visibility backstop: the CSS contrast computation is the first pass; what the browser actually painted is the backstop, in both renderings (JavaScript on and off). Pixels decide visibility, not AA: the backstop asks whether the text is actually painted, uncovered and readable at all; AA (4.5:1, 3:1 for large text) stays with the CSS pass whenever the CSS pass resolves the colours, and with axe and the manual WCAG review. Targets are every rendered text node (display:contents wrappers included), every native control showing text (value, placeholder or chosen option) and every named element without painted text of its own (accessible names only, hit test only). Text is measured line by line: each text node's word ranges are grouped into line boxes, so one hidden line of a paragraph is found. The scan is directed at targets, not fixed bands: after a first look at the top of the page, every line not yet measured is scrolled into view (its scrollable ancestors, such as an app shell's main panel or a tab strip, first; then the document) and measured once finite animations have finished and the painted state of every element (opacity, transform, filter, clip-path, colours, position) is unchanged about 100 ms apart, so CSS and script-driven entrances and scroll reveals are measured at rest (bounded at 2 s per step; elements under infinite animations are left out). The page bottom is then collected again so content added while scrolling is found, for at most four passes; a scan that stops at its step bound (300) or still finds new content after the last pass is INCOMPLETE. (a) A hit test at three points per line checks that the text, its own subtree or an ancestor is on top. An opaque cover (a replaced element, or a background colour at 90% or more opacity) at more than half the points obscures the line; a translucent cover (a lighter background colour, a background image or gradient, a backdrop filter, other text) is compositing the CSS pass cannot see, so that line is graded by its pixels. (b) Pixels: from a viewport screenshot after the page settles, over the middle 60% of the line box, the background is the modal colour, ink pixels differ from it by more than 24 in a channel, the readability ratio is the 90th percentile of the ink pixels' contrast against the background and the peak is the strongest ink pixel. Where the CSS pass resolved the colours and no translucent layer covers the line, the line fails only when it is effectively not painted: fewer than three ink pixels or a peak under 1.5:1 (colours that resolve to at least 3:1 but paint under 1.5:1 are hidden by an effect the CSS pass cannot see; light and thin weights that pass AA by colour are left to the CSS pass). Where the CSS pass could not resolve the colours, the pixels grade readability: under 3:1 is unreadable (under 1.5:1 for a line with fewer than 30 ink pixels, such as a single thin glyph). Rendered text under 4 px high is unreadable. Text that is obscured or unreadable with JavaScript on fails; text that is readable with JavaScript on but obscured, unreadable, unmeasurable or hidden with it off is an integrity mismatch, and a reviewed `jsOffDifferences` entry for it never makes it PASS. Text clipped to nothing by an ancestor that cannot be scrolled (an inactive carousel slide, an accordion collapsed with max-height:0), the same in both renderings, is not displayed: it is counted (`textHidden`) like display:none and graded through the declared states that show it. Text the backstop cannot measure (no box, outside the document, too few pixels, not reachable by scrolling, not reached before the bound) is INCOMPLETE, never PASS. Hide inactive content with `hidden`, `display:none` or `visibility:hidden`; visually hidden assistive text (the same sr-only rule as the width screen), an off-screen skip link that appears on focus, and the text of a disabled control (no contrast requirement in WCAG 1.4.3) are counted, not graded. Native authored strings (button-type input values, placeholders, option labels and accessible names) are copy: they are matched to the copy deck and screened for placeholders and filler like visible text, and a control's displayed value and its accessible name are compared separately with JavaScript off. The copy deck approves the displayed text and the accessible name separately (a slot's optional `ariaLabel`); a name identical to the displayed value is one string.
 
